// Chapter 4, "Third Parties." Sources read for this chapter (verified 2026-10-16):
// ISACA Certification Exam Candidate Guide, version 1.26 (2026), Appendix A, CISA Examination Content Outline (effective August
//   2024): topics 2A4 enterprise architecture; 2A6 privacy program and principles; 2A7 data governance and classification;
//   2B1 IT resource management; 2B2 IT vendor management; 2B4 quality assurance and quality management of IT. Quiz items 13 (1B2,
//   stratified sampling) and 14 (4A11, transactions) are spaced review of chapter 2:
//   https://www.isaca.org/-/media/files/isacadp/project/isaca/certification/exam-candidate-guides/2026/exam-candidate-guide.pdf
//   https://www.isaca.org/credentialing/cisa/cisa-exam-content-outline
// Mandatory leave: FDIC Risk Management Manual of Examination Policies, section 4.2, Internal Routine and Controls, "Vacation
//   Policies" (absent from duties for an uninterrupted period of not less than two consecutive weeks; vacation, rotation of duties,
//   or both; effective against embezzlement, which usually needs the perpetrator's ongoing presence):
//   https://www.fdic.gov/resources/supervision-and-examinations/examination-policies-manual/section4-2.pdf
// Service provider oversight under the Gramm Leach Bliley Act: FTC Safeguards Rule, 16 CFR 314.4(f) (select providers capable of
//   appropriate safeguards; require them by contract; periodically assess them): https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/16/314.4
//   and the Act's Financial Privacy Rule and Safeguards Rule: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gramm%E2%80%93Leach%E2%80%93Bliley_Act
//   (the lesson names the Act and what the rules expect, and doesn't say which agency's version applies to Meridian).
// Privacy principles (notice, choice and consent, access, security, enforcement; the OECD guidelines as the most cited version):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fair_information_practice
// ISO 9001 is the standard in the ISO 9000 family that organizations are certified against by third party assessment:
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_9000_family
// Zachman Framework (a two dimensional grid of the questions what, how, when, who, where, why against stages of reification):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zachman_Framework ; TOGAF (The Open Group; business, application, data, technology):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Open_Group_Architecture_Framework
// Boulder Valley School District 2026-27 calendar: spring break March 15 to 19, 2027, no school (bvsd.org/calendar, read
//   2026-10-16), so Monday's coffee falls in Maisie's spring break and March 29 to April 1 is a school week.
// Names: "Pellridge" (Pellridge Data Services, Inc., fictional) returned no web results on 2026-10-16 and appears in no course
//   bible or chapter.
// Dates: Monday, March 15 and Tuesday, March 16, 2027. US daylight saving started Sunday, March 14; the UK changes on March 28.
//   So Denver is UTC minus 6 and six hours behind London: 6:12 AM in Denver is 12:12 PM in London, 8 AM is 2 PM, and Precious's
//   8 PM London call lands at 2 PM in Denver. March 29, 2027 is a Monday and April 1 a Thursday.
// Figures (drill, re-derived): Pellridge 2026 fees $3,960,000 / Meridian 2026 IT spend $11,000,000 = 36%; SLA 99.95% over a
//   30 day month = 43,200 minutes x 0.0005 = 21.6 minutes allowed; Pellridge's own February 2027 report, 9 minutes down in 28
//   days = 40,320 minutes, availability (40,320 minus 9) / 40,320 = 99.978%, shown as 99.98. Archive line $8,400 a month.
// Facts set here for the ch4 lab and later chapters: Pellridge Data Services, Inc., Denver, formed 2019; contract July 2019, signed
//   for Meridian by C. Wynn; administrator access to the settlement release service; no right to audit, no SOC report, no vendor
//   review; vendor register says "Related party: No"; its registered agent also serves a holding company whose filings name a Kade
//   family trust as member (31 entities share the agent); ownership not established. Other four production vendors have audit
//   rights and SOC 2 Type 2 reports. ISO 9001 certificate scope: merchant onboarding and customer support, valid to June 2028.
//   Classification register: settlement files and merchant bank details INTERNAL, owner CIO, set by IT in 2019. Target architecture
//   (Architecture Review Board, October 2019): third party connections to end at the integration gateway by Q4 2020; board last met
//   February 2020. Fed totals delayed: Meridian's bank wants a request signed by Meridian. Kade's board meets Thursday, March 18.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cisa=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa.full||[]).push({
  num: 4, part: `Part One: The Folder`, title: `Third Parties`, weeks: `Monday, March 15 to Tuesday, March 16`,
  tasks: [`2A6`,`2A7`,`2B1`,`2B2`,`2B4`,`2A4`],
  cast: [
    [`Simon Kade`,`Founder, chairman, and chief executive of Meridian Clearing. Charming, generous, and very glad to meet you.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `The clocks had gone forward early on Sunday, and Henry had spent the lost hour the way he spent most of his hours now, at the desk on Wazee Street with the radiator knocking. On Monday morning London was six hours ahead instead of seven. It felt like a small favor from somebody who didn't know him.`,
    `Giles had written at 6:12, which was lunchtime at One Lime Street.`,
    `<prop type="email" from="Giles Fenwick" to="Henry Ashby" subject="The board" time="6:12 AM">Board read your interim view aloud, start to finish. Reserve stands. No decision on payment until your report. Their chairman rang ours at home on Saturday to say how much he's looking forward to meeting you. Ours didn't enjoy it. G.</prop>`,
    `Their chairman was Simon Kade, who was also their chief executive, and whose assistant had written on Saturday morning, through counsel this time, offering lunch at Meridian on Monday at half past twelve. Vellacott's counsel had said yes on his behalf, with rules. Meridian's general counsel would be there, and so would she. It was a courtesy. Nobody would discuss findings.`,
    `He looked Kade up while the kettle boiled. Meridian's website had a photograph of a tall man in an open collar, laughing at something just out of frame, and a paragraph about trust and speed that didn't say where he'd been before Meridian. He noticed it the way you notice a stair that isn't there.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Dot's Workpapers: other people's hands`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Somebody else is doing the work`,
        body: [`Every company you audit will tell you that some piece of the work belongs to somebody else, Lime Street. The payroll's with a provider. The servers are in somebody's cloud. The settlement files get handled by a firm across town. Fine. People outsource for good reasons: skills they don't have, cost, scale. What they can't outsource is the accountability. If the vendor loses the data, the company lost it. Write that on your hand.`,
          `So vendor management is a life cycle, and you audit each stage of it. Before the contract: why outsource at all, and is this vendor fit to do it? Due diligence means looking at the vendor's finances, its controls, its people, its own suppliers, and who owns it. Who owns it matters more than people think. A vendor that belongs to one of your directors is a related party. That contract should be disclosed and approved by people with no interest in it.`,
          `The contract is where the company keeps its rights, so read it the way you'd read a will. What's the service, and how is it measured? Service levels, with numbers. Is there a right to audit, or at least a promise of independent assurance, such as a SOC 2 Type 2 report? Then confidentiality, limits on how the vendor may use the data, a duty to report security incidents within a set time, rules for subcontracting, and the way out: how the data comes back or gets destroyed when it ends. For software the company can't live without, look for source code escrow.`,
          `Then monitoring, for as long as the contract runs. Somebody owns the relationship, reviews performance against the service levels, reads the assurance reports, and chases the exceptions. A service level report the vendor writes about itself is the vendor's opinion. Check it against the company's own records. And match the effort to the risk. The coffee machine people need a contract. A firm with administrator access to production needs everything above.`],
        terms: [[`Right to audit clause`,`A contract term that lets the customer, or auditors acting for it, examine the vendor's controls and records.`],[`Service level agreement (SLA)`,`The agreed, measurable standard of service, such as availability or response time, and what happens when it's missed.`],[`Vendor due diligence`,`Checking a vendor's capability, controls, finances, and ownership before contracting, and again on renewal.`],[`Related party`,`A party connected to the organization's directors or managers, such as a company one of them owns. Dealings with it need disclosure and independent approval.`]],
        exam: `Outsourcing moves the work, never the accountability. For a vendor that handles critical systems or sensitive data, the contract term the exam cares most about is the right to audit, or an agreed independent assurance report. Monitoring compares actual performance with the SLA, using evidence the vendor didn't write.` },
      { h: `People and money are resources too`,
        body: [`IT resource management is a dull name for how a company decides who does the work, what it costs, and what happens when somebody leaves. Start with people. Anyone in a sensitive job, employee or contractor, gets screened before they start. They have a job description that says what they may and may not do. When they go, their access goes the same day, and somebody checks that it went.`,
          `Then two controls that look like kindness and aren't. Mandatory leave means everyone in a sensitive job is away from it for a stretch, usually two weeks in a row, every year, while somebody else does the work. Job rotation moves people between duties on a schedule. They work for the same reason. Most fraud needs its author in the chair every day, answering the questions and keeping the story straight. Take them out of the chair and the story has to stand up without them. Bank examiners have pushed for it for longer than you've been alive. When somebody tells me proudly that nobody on the team has taken a holiday in years, I don't hear loyalty. I hear a control that isn't running.`,
          `Watch for key person dependency, where one person is the only one who knows how a system works. Cross training, written procedures, and a succession plan are the controls. The same risk turns up inside vendors, where the one person who understands your system works for somebody else.`,
          `Sourcing is a resource decision as well. In house, outsourced, or a mix should come out of a business case that weighs cost, skills, risk, and control. And the company should keep enough expertise of its own to manage the vendor, because a company that can't tell good work from bad can't manage anybody. Then the money: an IT budget tied to the strategy, actual spending compared with it, and costs charged back to the parts of the business that use the service, so they can see what it costs.`],
        terms: [[`Mandatory leave`,`A required, uninterrupted absence from a sensitive role, often two weeks a year, while someone else performs the duties.`],[`Job rotation`,`Moving staff between duties on a schedule, so no one holds a role long enough to hide a problem in it.`],[`Key person dependency`,`Reliance on one person's knowledge for a critical system or process.`],[`Chargeback`,`Allocating IT costs to the business units that use the services.`]],
        exam: `The main purpose of mandatory leave and job rotation is to detect errors and irregularities, because a substitute does the job. When one person holds all the knowledge of a critical system, cross training and documented procedures are the controls to pick.` },
      { h: `Whose data is it`,
        body: [`Data governance answers a question that sounds easy: who's in charge of this data? Every important set of data needs an owner, a named person in the business, usually a senior manager, who's accountable for it. The owner decides how sensitive it is, who may use it, and how long it's kept. IT is usually the custodian. The custodian runs the systems, takes the backups, and applies the controls the owner asks for. Users use it, within the rules. When IT names itself the owner, the people who know what the data is worth have been taken out of the decision, usually without noticing.`,
          `Classification is how the owner's judgment becomes controls. A company picks a short scheme, something like public, internal, confidential, and restricted, and says what each label requires: who can see it, whether it's encrypted, whether it may go to a vendor. Then every control keyed to the label inherits whatever mistake went into choosing it. A label set too low is a gate left open with a sign on it that says shut.`,
          `You can't classify what you haven't found, so the work starts with an inventory: what data the company holds, where every copy lives, including the copies at vendors, and who owns each one. Then the life cycle. Data gets created, used, shared, archived, and finally destroyed, under a retention schedule that meets the law and the business's needs and goes no further. Keeping data longer than you need it isn't caution. It's exposure. Data quality belongs here too: whether the data is accurate and complete enough for the decisions people make with it.`],
        terms: [[`Data owner`,`The business manager accountable for a data set, who classifies it and approves access to it.`],[`Data custodian`,`Usually IT. Stores and protects the data as the owner directs.`],[`Data classification`,`Labeling data by sensitivity and value so that each label carries required controls.`],[`Data inventory`,`A record of what data the organization holds, where every copy is, and who owns it.`]],
        exam: `The data owner classifies data and approves access. The custodian implements the protection. Before anything can be classified, the organization has to know what data it holds, where, and who owns it.` },
      { h: `Other people's names`,
        body: [`Privacy is about personal information: what a company collects about people, why, what it does with it, and who else gets it. Security is part of privacy. It isn't all of it. A company can keep a file perfectly safe and still be using it for something nobody agreed to.`,
          `The principles behind most privacy laws are old and short. Tell people what you collect and why. Give them a choice where they're owed one. Collect only what you need for the purpose you stated, and use it only for that purpose. Keep it accurate. Keep it no longer than you need. Protect it. Let people see and correct what you hold about them. And somebody is answerable for all of it.`,
          `A privacy program puts those principles to work. There's a person responsible, often called a privacy officer. There's a notice that tells people the truth. There's a map of the personal data and where it flows. And there's a privacy impact assessment before any new system, process, or vendor starts handling personal data, so the risks get dealt with in the design instead of in the apology.`,
          `Here's where the vendor comes back in. When a company tells its customers their data goes only to providers bound by contract to protect it, the contracts had better say so. In the United States, the Gramm Leach Bliley Act makes financial institutions protect their customers' nonpublic personal information, and the rules under it expect them to choose providers who can safeguard it, require the safeguards by contract, and check on them from time to time. A promise in a notice that never made it into a contract is a promise nobody kept.`,
          `And you handle personal data too, every time you test. Take only the fields your test needs. Mask what you can. Keep it safe while you have it, and get rid of it when the work allows.`],
        terms: [[`Data minimization`,`Collecting and keeping only the personal data needed for a stated purpose.`],[`Privacy impact assessment (PIA)`,`An assessment of how a new system, process, or vendor will affect personal data, done before it starts, so risks are handled in the design.`],[`Privacy notice`,`The organization's statement to individuals about what it collects, why, how it's used, and who it's shared with.`]],
        exam: `A privacy impact assessment is done before personal data is processed in a new way, ideally at design. A privacy notice is a commitment, so test whether contracts and practices actually deliver it.` },
      { h: `Quality isn't a plaque`,
        body: [`Quality assurance and quality control get used as if they meant the same thing. They don't. Quality control checks the product: this release, this report, this file. Quality assurance checks the process that makes the product, so good results come out by design instead of by luck. Both need some distance from the work. A QA team that reports to the manager whose work it tests will be asked, now and then, to be reasonable.`,
          `Plenty of companies run a quality management system against a standard, most often ISO 9001. An independent certification body audits it and, if it's satisfied, issues a certificate. That means something. But only within its scope, which is written on the certificate in one line most people never read. A certificate for customer support says nothing about the payment engine. Read the scope, read the dates, and if you need more, ask for the certification audit report.`,
          `Other models rate how mature a company's processes are, CMMI being the one you'll hear most, from ad hoc at the bottom to measured and continually improved at the top. Whatever the model, I look for the same things. Quality requirements written down and measurable. Reviews and tests before release, not after a customer complains. Defects traced to their causes, and the causes fixed. And vendors' work held to the same standard as the company's own. A company that tests its own code with care and takes a vendor's code on the vendor's word has half a quality system.`],
        terms: [[`Quality assurance (QA)`,`Activities that make sure processes produce quality results. About the process.`],[`Quality control (QC)`,`Checking outputs against requirements. About the product.`],[`ISO 9001`,`The international standard for quality management systems. Certification covers only the scope stated on the certificate.`]],
        exam: `QA is about the process and QC about the product. QA should be independent of the people whose work it reviews. When an answer leans on a certificate, check that its scope covers the area in question.` },
      { h: `The map and the ground`,
        body: [`Enterprise architecture is a company's map of itself: its business processes, the applications that support them, the data that moves between them, and the technology underneath. A good one has two drawings. The current state is what exists today. The target state is where the company has decided to go. The gap analysis between them becomes the plan, and somebody, usually an architecture review board, decides which projects close which gaps and checks that new work fits.`,
          `There are frameworks for drawing it. The Zachman Framework is a grid: what, how, where, who, when, and why, asked from each point of view, from the planner's down to the builder's. TOGAF, from The Open Group, works across four domains, business, data, application, and technology, with a method for getting from the current state to the target. You don't need to draw either. You need to know what they're for.`,
          `I use architecture to find things. The map shows every system, every interface, and every door to the outside, and your job is to check the map against the ground. Lay the documented connections beside the access lists and the network rules. Anything on the ground that isn't on the map is either a mistake in the drawing or something nobody wanted drawn. Either way it's a finding.`,
          `Then compare the target with what happened. A target state approved years ago and never reached is a decision made by default, by whoever didn't do the work. Ask who accepted the gap. Usually nobody did. That's the finding too.`],
        terms: [[`Current and target state`,`The architecture as it exists, and the architecture the organization has decided to move to.`],[`Gap analysis`,`A comparison of the current state with the target state, which becomes the roadmap.`],[`Zachman Framework`,`An enterprise architecture framework laid out as a grid of perspectives against the questions what, how, where, who, when, and why.`],[`TOGAF`,`The Open Group's architecture framework, covering business, data, application, and technology architecture.`]],
        exam: `Gap analysis compares the current state with the target state. Enterprise architecture should support business strategy and give an accurate, current picture of systems, data flows, and interfaces, including third party connections.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f4s1`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2B2`, title:`Fourteen vendors`, q:`Pellridge's register row says none three times. What should Henry do about it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[(G.hidconflict
      ?`At eight he rang London for the word Giles had promised him on Friday. Compliance was a woman with a quiet voice and a very good memory for dates. She went through Tuesday with him, hour by hour, and wrote down when he'd known about Dot and when he'd told anyone. Then she told him what would happen. He'd stay on the claim, because the claim couldn't wait. Every workpaper would go to Priyanka for review before it left his desk. And a note would go on his file, saying the disclosure had come from Meridian's lawyers, three days late. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"That's fair,"</span> he said. She said it wasn't meant to be fair. It was meant to be written down.`
      :`At eight he read through his own disclosure note from Tuesday, the one that had sent Meridian's lawyers away on Friday in four minutes. It was dull, and dated, and it said exactly what he'd known and when. He'd never been so fond of a dull document.`),
        `At 8:10 counsel rang. The Federal Reserve totals for March 2 to 5 wouldn't come today. Meridian's bank wanted the request signed by Meridian itself, and Meridian's general counsel was "reviewing the form." Henry wrote the delay in the file with the date and who had caused it, because a delay is evidence too. Then he sent Giles two lines, since the interim view had promised him the totals today: they'd slipped, and here was why.`,`At 8:40 he went through Meridian's answer to a request he'd made on Wednesday: every outside company that touched the settlement service, with its contract. There were fourteen. Most sold Meridian the things any office buys, a phone system, a payroll service, the coffee machines. Five had access to production. He read their rows from the bottom up.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian Clearing, vendor register (extract, production access)">Pellridge Data Services, Inc., Denver. Service: settlement operations support and file archive.<br>Access: production, administrator, settlement release service.<br>Contract: July 2019, signed for Meridian by C. Wynn, CFO. Renews yearly.<br>Right to audit: none. Independent assurance report: none on file. Last vendor review: none recorded. Related party: No.<br><br>Other vendors with production access (4): right to audit, yes. SOC 2 Type 2 on file, yes.</prop>`,`Four rows that looked the way rows should, and one that didn't.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ring Pellridge and ask them to send their controls documents today.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`A pleasant man at Pellridge says he'll need to check with Meridian, and does, inside a minute. By ten Meridian's general counsel has written to Vellacott's counsel asking why the insurer's investigator is ringing their suppliers.`,
          why:`The insured's vendors are reached through the channel the engagement sets. And documents a vendor sends about itself, on request, are its own assertions.`},
        {t:`Ask for Pellridge's SOC 2 report, since the other four have one.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Counsel asks. The answer comes back by noon: Pellridge has never had one, and Meridian never asked for one. It's a useful fact. It's one line of the problem.`,
          why:`Independent assurance is one way to oversee a vendor, and asking for it is reasonable. But the gap is wider than one report: no audit right, no assurance, and no review, for a vendor holding administrator access to production.`},
        {t:`Note Meridian can show no oversight of a vendor with admin access.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it as a condition: a vendor with administrator access to the settlement release service, no right to audit, no independent assurance, and no review since 2019. Under it he writes the criteria the way Dot would have. The company that outsources a service keeps the accountability for it. Then he asks counsel for Pellridge's due diligence file from 2019, and for anything at all showing that someone at Meridian ever checked Pellridge's work.`,
          why:`Outsourcing moves the work, not the accountability. Meridian should have done due diligence before signing, kept a right to audit or obtained independent assurance, and monitored the vendor in proportion to its access. Record what's missing, then ask for whatever would prove you wrong.`},
        {t:`Conclude Pellridge is how the intruder got in, and tell Giles.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`Giles would be thrilled. Henry has a missing clause and a hunch, and no evidence that Pellridge's access was used at all.`,
          why:`A control gap is a weakness. It isn't proof of how a loss happened. Report the gap, and keep your theory out of the finding until evidence supports it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s2`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A4`, title:`Page 44`, q:`How should Henry use Meridian's architecture diagram from page 44?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At twenty past ten he opened the claim pack to page 44, where Meridian had drawn its settlement architecture for the insurer: tidy boxes, arrows, a legend in the corner.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Settlement architecture, current state (claim pack, page 44)">Merchant portal, to settlement application, to release gateway (dual authorization), to the Federal Reserve.<br>Monitoring: DOME, all components.<br>Third party connections: shown at the edge of each component.</prop>`,`He laid the vendor register beside it. Four of the five vendors with production access were drawn in, as small gray boxes at the edges. Pellridge, with administrator access to the release service, wasn't on the page at all.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Trust the diagram. Meridian drew it for the insurer, and it's recent.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It's recent and it's tidy. It's also missing a company with administrator rights to the box in the middle.`,
          why:`An architecture diagram is management's description of its environment. Compare it with independent sources, such as access lists and connection inventories, before you rely on it.`},
        {t:`Reconcile the drawing to the access list, and log each link it omits.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He does it line by line, every vendor with production access against every connection on page 44. Pellridge is the only one missing. He writes the condition and the source of each half, then asks counsel for Meridian's interface inventory and its architecture documents, current and planned, with their approval dates. If the drawing and the list disagree, one of them is wrong, and the company ought to know which.`,
          why:`Enterprise architecture should give an accurate picture of systems, data flows, interfaces, and third party connections. Testing the documented architecture against an independent inventory finds what's missing, and an undocumented connection into production is a finding in itself.`},
        {t:`Ask Leonard to send an updated diagram showing all vendor links.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Leonard sends one by two o'clock, with Pellridge drawn in, in a slightly different gray. It's a new drawing made after the question. It tells him nothing about what Meridian knew before he asked.`,
          why:`A corrected document helps the future. It isn't evidence of the architecture as Meridian documented and governed it. Record the gap in the original first.`},
        {t:`Sketch his own map of the real environment, and work from that.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`He spends an hour on it. It's his picture of what he thinks is there, built from one list Meridian sent him, and it isn't Meridian's architecture or evidence of how Meridian governed it.`,
          why:`Your own reconstruction can't stand in for the organization's architecture. Report the gap between what they documented and what they run.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s3`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2B4`, title:`Lit like a painting`, q:`Meridian's lobby has an ISO 9001 certificate lit like a painting. What should Henry make of it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Meridian had the top two floors of a glass tower on Seventeenth Street. At 12:25 Henry stood in the lobby with Vellacott's counsel, who'd come to make sure lunch stayed lunch, and looked at the wall behind the reception desk. It held one framed certificate, lit like a painting.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Certificate of registration, Meridian Clearing reception">ISO 9001:2015. Meridian Clearing, Inc.<br>Quality management system.<br>Scope: merchant onboarding and customer support services.<br>Valid until June 2028.</prop>`,`The receptionist saw him reading it. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Mr. Kade's very proud of that one,"</span> she said.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Note the certificate as evidence of quality over settlement.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He writes it down. That evening, reading his notes back, he sees that the word settlement isn't on the certificate anywhere.`,
          why:`An ISO 9001 certificate covers a quality management system within its stated scope. It's evidence about that scope only, and it isn't evidence that a particular control works.`},
        {t:`Read the scope line. Settlement isn't in it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`He copies the scope into his notebook word for word. The certificate is real, and probably earned. It covers onboarding and customer support, the parts of Meridian that face its customers, and stops well short of the part that moves the money. He'll ask counsel for the latest certification audit report to see what was looked at, but he already knows what wasn't.`,
          why:`Certification is only as wide as its scope. Read the scope statement and the dates, and if you need more, ask for the certification body's audit report. A quality certificate for one process says nothing about another.`},
        {t:`Ask for the certification body's latest audit report first.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Counsel adds it to the list. It's a good request. He could have saved a week by reading the line under the company's name.`,
          why:`The audit report would help. But the first thing to read was on the wall in front of you, and the scope already says the certificate doesn't cover settlement.`},
        {t:`Dismiss it. A certificate on a lobby wall is just marketing.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`He turns his back on it. It was real, and it said exactly where Meridian's quality system stopped. He's just walked past the most honest thing in the lobby.`,
          why:`ISO certification involves an independent audit and means something within its scope. Don't dismiss evidence. Read what it covers.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s4`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2B1`, title:`Loyalty`, q:`Kade calls his team's habit loyalty. How should Henry respond?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Simon Kade came down to collect them himself, which chairmen don't usually do. He was tall and easy, with a good handshake and a better memory. He'd read up on Henry, or had somebody do it for him, and he asked about the Intelligence Corps as if it were a school they'd both nearly gone to.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kade">"I was sorry about Dorothy Kessler,"</span> he said in the lift, before anyone else could say anything. <span class="said" data-who="Kade">"She examined us for nine years. Terrifying woman. I liked her enormously."</span> Henry said thank you, and felt Vellacott's counsel not look at him.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kade">"She had a name for everyone, did you know? Nine years, and I was never once Simon. I was the Chairman, said like a diagnosis."</span> He smiled as if they had that in common. <span class="said" data-who="Kade">"I expect she had one for you."</span>`,`Henry watched the floor numbers climb and didn't tell him. It was the one thing of hers nobody had put in an evidence bag.`,`Lunch was in a corner room with the whole Front Range in the window. Meridian's general counsel sat at the end of the table and wrote things down. Kade ate almost nothing and talked well.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kade">"Here's the secret of this company, Mr. Ashby. We're small. Eleven people run settlement for more banks than you'd believe. In 2019 we handed the machinery to Pellridge, good people, old friends, and IT went from sixteen to five. And my settlement team? Nobody's taken a full week off since 2019. They won't. I've tried to make them."</span> He smiled. <span class="said" data-who="Kade">"You can't buy that kind of loyalty."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell Kade it's admirable. Loyal staff are worth a great deal.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Kade looks pleased. Meridian's general counsel writes something down, and it isn't about loyalty.`,
          why:`To an auditor, staff who never take leave aren't evidence of loyalty. Unbroken presence in a key role removes one of the simplest ways irregularities come to light.`},
        {t:`Ask Kade which of his team actually approve the settlement files.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Kade says he'd have to check, which is charming and unlikely, and the general counsel says requests go through counsel. Henry has asked the right question of the wrong person, across a lunch table.`,
          why:`Who holds the approver role matters. But ask for records, through the agreed channel. A name from the chief executive's memory is inquiry, and he isn't the one who keeps the records.`},
        {t:`Explain that banks expect two weeks' absence a year, and why.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Kade listens with real interest and thanks him. Whatever records Henry asks for next, Meridian now knows why he wants them, and has three weeks to think about it.`,
          why:`Teaching the auditee about a weakness in the middle of fieldwork, before you have the records, tells them what you'll test. Note it, request the evidence, and report it.`},
        {t:`Say nothing to Kade. Note it, and request the records through counsel.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He nods and lets Kade talk. Under the table, in his notebook, he writes: <b>No enforced absence in settlement roles since 2019 (CEO, verbal). IT outsourced to Pellridge 2019, staff 16 to 5 (CEO, verbal). Request leave records, job descriptions, staffing plan, through counsel.</b> Kade's words are inquiry, the weakest evidence there is. They're also an excellent map of where to look.`,
          why:`Mandatory leave and job rotation are old banking controls, because most fraud needs its author in the chair every day. A small team that never steps away and relies on a vendor's staff is a resource management risk. Treat the remark as inquiry and confirm it with records.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s5`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A6`, title:`Worth a try`, q:`Kade offers Meridian's whole customer file in exchange for Henry's findings so far. What should Henry say?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Over coffee Kade leaned back in his chair.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kade">"Here's what I'd like to do. You're getting what you need from my lawyers a teaspoon at a time, and I'm sorry about that. So I'll have the team put our whole customer file on a drive for you this afternoon. Every merchant, every account, every cardholder. The lot. Then you've got everything."</span> He turned his cup on its saucer. <span class="said" data-who="Kade">"And in return, just tell me what you've found so far. If something's broken, I'd rather my board heard it from me on Thursday than from anyone else."</span>`,`Meridian's general counsel studied the ceiling.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him about the Pellridge contract. He's the man who can fix it.`, s:0, flag:`toldkade`, d:{trust:-5,conf:0,health:-6},
          after:`Kade listens, nods slowly, and says, <span class="said" data-who="Kade">"Thank you. That's exactly what I'd want to know."</span> Vellacott's counsel puts her pen down very carefully. In the lift Henry says, <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Sorry."</span> She says, <span class="said" data-who="woman">"We'll talk about it,"</span> and they do, all the way to the car.`,
          why:`You told the insured's chief executive what the insurer's investigation had found, before it was reported and while the facts were still being tested. That lets the auditee change the record and shape its answers. Findings go to your client, through the reporting channel.`},
        {t:`Accept the drive. With every record in hand, nothing gets missed.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`At five a courier brings a drive holding the names and bank details of several million people. Henry now has to store it, protect it, and explain why he needed it. He didn't.`,
          why:`Collect only the personal data your objective needs. Taking more creates a privacy duty and a security risk for you, and does nothing for the test.`},
        {t:`Decline both, and say he'll hear the findings with his board.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Kade takes it well. But Henry has just promised Meridian's board a briefing that isn't his to give. His findings belong to Vellacott, and Vellacott decides who hears them, and when.`,
          why:`Turning down both is right. Promising when and to whom the findings will go isn't yours to decide. Your work goes to the people who engaged you.`},
        {t:`Ask for only the fields the claim needs, via counsel. Share no findings.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"That's kind,"</span> Henry says. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Counsel will send the list of fields I need. Names and card numbers won't be on it. And what I've found so far goes to London first, I'm afraid."</span> Kade laughs as if he'd been beaten fairly at something. <span class="said" data-who="Kade">"Ah, well. Worth a try."</span> The general counsel stops studying the ceiling.`,
          why:`Data minimization means taking only the personal data a purpose needs, and an auditor is bound by it like anyone else. And findings go to the client through the agreed channel, never to the auditee's chief executive over coffee.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s6`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A7`, title:`The label decides the gate`, q:`Settlement files are labeled Internal, which lets them go to any approved vendor. What should Henry make of that?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Agent Obi rang at five past three. The bureau had the claim file now, she said, and then she asked what she'd actually rung about. <span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"Did he mention her?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"He said he liked her enormously."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"Did you believe him?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I don't know yet."</span>`,`She let that go. <span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"Your father was at Lloyd's too."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Was. He's retired."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"Is he well?"</span>`,`He looked at the window for a while. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"No."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"I'm sorry,"</span> she said, and sounded it, and rang off.`,`He hadn't told her about his father. He thought about ringing back to ask how she knew, and opened the classification register instead.`,`It was short.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Data classification register (extract)">Settlement files: INTERNAL. Merchant bank account details: INTERNAL.<br>Data owner: CIO. Classified: 2019, by IT. Next review: 2020.<br><br>From the classification standard: RESTRICTED data may go to a vendor only with the data owner's written approval and encryption. INTERNAL data may go to any approved vendor.</prop>`,`So the label decided the gate. And the people who'd chosen the label were the people who ran the systems.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Accept the labels as they are. IT manages the data, so IT knows it best.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He writes "classification per Meridian." It means the people who looked after the data also decided how much it mattered, and nobody who'd lose money if it leaked was asked.`,
          why:`IT is usually the custodian: it runs the systems and applies controls. Deciding how sensitive data is belongs to the business owner, who understands what its loss would cost.`},
        {t:`Reclassify the settlement files as Restricted in his own workpapers.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`It's almost certainly the right label. It also isn't his to set, and his file now holds a classification nobody at Meridian ever made.`,
          why:`You can say a classification looks wrong, and why. Setting it is the owner's job. Report the gap and let the owner decide.`},
        {t:`Note IT set the labels, and the low label skipped owner sign off.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes the chain out in order. The CIO's team, the custodian, set the labels in 2019 and named itself owner. Settlement files went down as Internal. Internal data can go to any approved vendor without the owner's sign off, so Pellridge never needed anyone's sign off. Nobody in settlement operations ever chose that risk. He adds a request for whoever in the business should have owned the data.`,
          why:`Data owners in the business classify data and approve access to it. Custodians in IT protect it as the owner directs. When the custodian classifies, the label tends to follow convenience, and every control keyed to the label inherits the mistake.`},
        {t:`Ask the CIO to confirm, in writing, that each of the labels is correct.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The CIO confirms by morning, in two lines. It's the same team marking its own homework again.`,
          why:`Confirmation from whoever made the decision isn't independent evidence. Compare the labels with the classification standard and ask who should own the data.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s7`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2B1`, title:`Pearl Street`, q:`Laura asks Henry to take Maisie for four days, school runs included. What should he say?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The coffee place on Pearl Street was next door to the bookshop, and at seven on a Monday in spring break it held students, a man with a guitar case, and Laura, at a table by the window with two cups. She'd already bought his. It was the right one. He decided not to make anything of that.`,(G.missedfair
        ?`<span class="said" data-who="Laura">"One thing about the fair, and then I'm done with it. She asked me on Saturday if you'd seen the poster. I said I'd send you a photo. I'm not going to. Ask her."</span> And then she was done with it, which was worse than if she hadn't been.`
        :`<span class="said" data-who="Laura">"She's put the poster on her bedroom door,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Laura">"With the error in the corner. She tells people it's a real analysis."</span>`),`<span class="said" data-who="Laura">"Here's the ask. My husband's father is having surgery in Duluth the week of my defense. It was booked before my date was set, and he has to go. So I need you to have Maisie from Monday the twenty ninth to Thursday the first. School run included. That's Denver to Boulder by eight, and back for her at three thirty, four days."</span> She turned her cup a quarter turn. <span class="said" data-who="Laura">"I don't want a yes. I want a true answer."</span>`,`He opened his mouth and she held up a hand.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Laura">"Before you answer. I'm going to say this once. When you were in the Army I was married to a man who noticed everything and told me nothing. In 2014 I found a hospital wristband in your kit bag. A field hospital. Your name on it, and a date, and it was the week you rang and said everything was boring."</span> She didn't look away. <span class="said" data-who="Laura">"I never asked. You never said. I put it back in the pocket and I washed the bag. I'm not asking now. I'm telling you what it was like. And I've been the one in the middle, between you and her, for eight years. I'd like to stop."</span> She let that sit. <span class="said" data-who="Laura">"If it's a no, say no. Don't say yes and go quiet. I can plan around no."</span>`,`He remembered the week. He'd never once thought about the bag. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I didn't know you'd found it,"</span> he said.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Laura">"I know."</span> She turned the cup back the other way. <span class="said" data-who="Laura">"That was the whole problem."</span>`,`Then he looked at the case in his head the way he'd look at a schedule, because she'd asked for a true answer and that was where it was. The week of the twenty ninth would be the end of it, one way or another.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Say yes, straight away. She needs to hear yes, and he'll make it work.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-4,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Yes."</span> Laura nods. He watches her start to plan around it and then stop, because she's heard that yes before, and she knows where it goes.`,
          why:`A commitment made without looking at what could get in the way is a hope. Say what you can deliver, and what could stop you, before you promise.`},
        {t:`Say probably, and promise to confirm when the case settles.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Laura">"When's that?"</span> He doesn't know. She puts the dates in her phone anyway, and he can see she's already working out a plan that doesn't need him.`,
          why:`It's honest, but it leaves her holding the uncertainty with no date for an answer. A useful commitment says what you'll do, what could change it, and when the other person will know.`},
        {t:`Ask whether her husband could fly back midweek for the runs.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-5,health:0},
          after:`Laura looks at him for a long moment. <span class="said" data-who="Laura">"His father's having surgery, Henry."</span> She doesn't say anything else. She doesn't need to.`,
          why:`That hands the problem back to the person who brought it, when she came to you because she'd run out of other people. Look at what you can carry first.`},
        {t:`Yes. Then say what could go wrong that week, and his plan if it does.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:6,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Yes. And here's what could go wrong. That's probably the last week of this case. My phone will ring at odd hours, and London may want me at six in the morning. So tonight I'll tell Giles I'm not available before nine or after three that week, and he can plan round it. Every school run is mine. If anything threatens one, you'll hear it from me by eight the night before, with a fix."</span> She turns over the page in front of her, the running order for her defense with the committee's names down the side, and writes it on the back, all of it, including the eight. Then she folds it into her coat pocket the way you'd keep a receipt. <span class="said" data-who="Laura">"Okay,"</span> she says.`,
          why:`An honest commitment of resources starts from real capacity: what's already booked, what could pull you away, and the backup if it does. Telling the people who'll compete for your time, early, is what makes the promise one you can keep.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s8`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2B2`, title:`Thirty one companies`, q:`Pellridge shares a registered agent with a company owned by a Kade family trust. How should Henry handle it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[...(((s.picks||{})[6]===3||(s.picks||{})[6]===0)
        ?[`At 7:52 on Tuesday morning his phone buzzed. <b>mum says im at yours the week of the 29th. do you have a toaster</b> He looked at his kitchen. He wrote back <b>I will by then</b>, and she sent a thumbs up. He took it.`]
        :[]),`At half past eight he did something nobody had asked him to do, and looked Pellridge up in the Colorado Secretary of State's business records, which anyone can search for free.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Henry's notes: public business filings">Pellridge Data Services, Inc. Formed 2019. Registered agent: a law office suite on Seventeenth Street.<br>Same registered agent, same suite: 31 entities.<br>One of them: a holding company whose filings name its member as a Kade family trust.</prop>`,`Registered agents serve dozens of companies, sometimes hundreds. It's a business. He knew that. He also knew Meridian's vendor register had a column headed Related party. Over lunch Kade had called them good people, old friends. The register said No.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell Giles that Kade secretly owns Pellridge. The filings make it plain.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`They don't. They show two companies that pay the same agent, one of which belongs to a Kade trust. That's thirty one companies' worth of coincidence until somebody shows who owns what.`,
          why:`A shared registered agent is a lead. It isn't proof of ownership, and overstating it exposes your client and your report.`},
        {t:`Log a possible undisclosed related party, and test it through counsel.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it carefully: shared registered agent; register says not related; ownership not established. Then the tests, through counsel. Meridian's related party disclosures. The directors' conflict of interest declarations. Who approved the 2019 contract, and on what due diligence. Pellridge's owners, as Meridian recorded them when it took Pellridge on. If the answer's innocent, those papers will show it. If it isn't, they'll show that too.`,
          why:`Vendor due diligence covers who you're contracting with, including conflicts of interest and related parties, which need disclosure and independent approval. A shared agent justifies further testing. It doesn't justify a conclusion.`},
        {t:`Ask Leonard if internal audit ever looked at who owns Pellridge.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Leonard says internal audit hasn't, and sounds as if he's only now wondering why. It's a fair question with a sad answer, and it tells Henry nothing about who owns Pellridge.`,
          why:`Internal audit's coverage is worth knowing. But the evidence of ownership and approval sits in the disclosures, declarations, and contract approvals. Go there.`},
        {t:`Drop it. Registered agents serve many firms, so it means nothing.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He closes the tab. A week later Priyanka asks him why the vendor with administrator access and no audit clause never got an ownership check, and he doesn't have a good answer.`,
          why:`A shared agent is weak evidence on its own. Beside a vendor with production access, no audit clause, and a "not related" box, it's enough to justify a few cheap tests.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s9`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A6`, title:`Nine pages`, q:`Meridian's published privacy policy and the Pellridge agreement say different things about data. What should Henry do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`He'd had the Pellridge contract since Monday morning and hadn't read past the signature block. At ten to ten he read all nine pages, then pulled up Meridian's privacy notice from its own website, the one every merchant agreed to when it signed up.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian privacy notice (website, extract), and Henry's notes on the Pellridge agreement">Notice: "We share your information only with service providers who are bound by contract to keep it confidential and to use it only to provide services to us."<br><br>Pellridge master services agreement, July 2019: services, service levels, fees, term, renewal, limitation of liability. Confidentiality: not addressed. Use of data: not addressed. Notice of security incidents: not addressed.</prop>`,`He looked for a privacy impact assessment from 2019, when Pellridge started handling merchants' bank details. Meridian's privacy file held nothing for that year.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Conclude Meridian broke federal privacy law, and say so in the report.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Perhaps it did. Henry isn't a lawyer, and "broke the law" is a conclusion for people who are.`,
          why:`Report the condition and the criteria you can support, and refer legal conclusions to counsel.`},
        {t:`Ask Meridian's privacy officer to explain how Pellridge got approved.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Counsel sends the question. The explanation will be worth having. In the meantime the notice and the contract are sitting side by side on his desk, saying different things, and he hasn't written that down.`,
          why:`Asking is reasonable, and you'll want the explanation. But the documents already show the gap between the notice and the contract. Record it now.`},
        {t:`Log the gap: the notice promises terms the contract never imposed.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it in three parts. Meridian told merchants their data would go only to providers bound by contract to keep it confidential and use it only for Meridian. The contract that sent their bank details to Pellridge says neither. No privacy impact assessment was done before it started. Then a fourth line, for the claim: nothing obliged Pellridge to tell Meridian about a security incident, or when.`,
          why:`A privacy program makes sure the organization does what its notice promises. That includes flowing privacy and security terms into vendor contracts and assessing privacy impact before personal data moves to a new party. A missing incident notice clause also matters for any claim's timeline.`},
        {t:`Recommend Meridian sign a new privacy addendum with Pellridge now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It's a good recommendation for next month. It does nothing about what the contract said in March, and the claim turns on March.`,
          why:`Record the condition first. Recommendations follow findings, and they can't change what was in force when the loss happened.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s10`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A7`, title:`Seven year retention`, q:`Pellridge's monthly invoices list a seven year archive. How should Henry treat it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Pellridge's invoices came next, a year of them. One line repeated every month: <b>Settlement file archive, seven year retention: $8,400.</b>`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian data inventory: settlement files">Locations: settlement application database; release gateway logs; DOME.<br>Retention: seven years, Meridian systems.<br>Disposal: Records team.</prop>`,`Pellridge wasn't on it. By the look of the invoices, a copy of every settlement file Meridian had sent since 2019 was sitting somewhere that Meridian's own inventory didn't know existed.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ignore it. An archive is only a backup, and backups aren't his concern.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He moves on. Years of other people's settlement data stay outside every rule Meridian has about data, and outside his report.`,
          why:`A copy of sensitive data is still sensitive data. Where it lives, who owns it, and how long it's kept are governance questions wherever the copy sits.`},
        {t:`Ask Pellridge directly to send him the archive so he can compare it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Counsel stops it before it leaves the building. Pellridge holds the data for Meridian, under a contract Henry isn't party to, and he'd be asking a vendor to hand an outsider years of other people's settlements.`,
          why:`Data held for the auditee is requested through the auditee and the agreed channel, and only as much as the test needs.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel to add the Pellridge archive to his data requests.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's a sensible request, and counsel sends it. But it treats the archive as a new source of data for him. The finding is that Meridian's own governance never knew it was there.`,
          why:`The archive may be useful evidence later. First record the governance gap: data outside the inventory, with no owner and no rules for keeping or destroying it.`},
        {t:`Record a copy of settlement data outside Meridian's inventory and rules.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it plainly: a copy of settlement data held by a third party since 2019, missing from the data inventory, with no contract terms on retention, return, or destruction, and no owner who knew. Then he rings counsel, because a copy nobody listed is also a copy nobody has preserved. She says she'll write to the bureau before lunch.`,
          why:`Data governance covers data through its whole life, wherever it sits: inventory, ownership, retention, and disposal, including copies held by vendors. Data that isn't in the inventory is outside every control that depends on it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s11`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2B4`, title:`Half a system`, q:`What should Henry conclude from Meridian's IT quality assurance charter?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Meridian's quality assurance charter arrived just after noon: two pages, and an organization chart with one box on it that mattered.`,`<prop type="doc" title="IT Quality Assurance, charter and reporting line (extract)">Staff: 2. Reports to: Head of Settlement Development.<br>Scope: release testing of the settlement application.<br>Vendor releases (Pellridge): accepted on the vendor's own test evidence.</prop>`,`Two people checked the settlement software, and both worked for the person who built it. Everything Pellridge changed was checked by Pellridge.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Accept the charter. A written QA function shows quality is managed.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He ticks the box. A charter shows that someone wrote a charter. It doesn't show who QA answers to, or whose work it never sees.`,
          why:`A document establishing QA isn't evidence that QA is effective. Look at its independence and its coverage.`},
        {t:`Note QA answers to the builders, and vendor work goes unchecked.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes two lines. QA reports to the head of the team whose releases it tests. And QA doesn't test Pellridge's releases at all. It takes Pellridge's word for them. Then a third, smaller: the ISO certificate in the lobby stopped at customer support, so nothing outside the company had looked at this either.`,
          why:`Quality assurance needs independence from the work it reviews, and its coverage should include work done by vendors. A QA team inside development, which accepts a vendor's own test evidence, gives assurance over half the system at best.`},
        {t:`Ask QA for its defect statistics for the last two years.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`They'll be useful. They'll also be the numbers of a team that reports to the people whose defects it counts, and they won't include a single Pellridge release.`,
          why:`Defect data is good evidence of how the process performs. Before you rely on it, settle whether the people producing it are independent, and what they never see.`},
        {t:`Recommend Meridian extend its ISO 9001 certificate to settlement.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It's a fine idea for 2028. It tells nobody anything about how settlement software was checked in 2027.`,
          why:`Recommendations come after findings. Establish what the QA arrangement was, and what it covered, first.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s12`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A4`, title:`By Q4 2020`, q:`Meridian's target architecture says vendors will connect through a gateway by late 2020. What should Henry do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At a quarter past one the architecture documents from his first request list came through counsel. The target architecture was older than the current one, which was the first thing wrong with it.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Settlement target architecture, approved by the Architecture Review Board, October 2019">"By Q4 2020, all third party connections to production will terminate at the integration gateway. No vendor will have direct access to the settlement release service."<br><br>Architecture Review Board minutes: last meeting held February 2020.</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Compare target with current, and find who accepted the gap.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He sets the two side by side. In October 2019 Meridian decided no vendor would reach the release service directly after 2020. In March 2027 one vendor does, with administrator rights. The board that was meant to watch the plan stopped meeting four months after approving it. He looks for anyone who ever decided to keep the direct connection. There's nobody. He writes that down too, because nobody is an answer.`,
          why:`Comparing current state with the approved target shows where the plan stalled. A gap nobody accepted is a decision made by default, and an architecture review board that stopped meeting is a governance failure in itself.`},
        {t:`Treat the target as the current state. The board approved it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`On paper, Pellridge was cut off from the release service at the end of 2020. He writes that down, and it's the opposite of what the vendor register says.`,
          why:`A target state is a plan, not a fact. Test what exists today against it.`},
        {t:`Ask the CIO when the move to the gateway will be finished.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`The CIO says it's "on the roadmap." It's a fair question, and the answer tells him the plan is still a plan, six years on. It doesn't tell him who let it slide.`,
          why:`Asking about status is reasonable. The finding is in the gap itself, and in the absence of anyone who accepted it.`},
        {t:`Draw up a new target architecture he thinks Meridian should adopt.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It's a good diagram. It's also consulting, not auditing, and it'll be in his file next to a finding he hasn't written.`,
          why:`Designing the auditee's architecture isn't the auditor's job, and doing it can impair independence. Report the gap and let management decide how to close it.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f4s8`, title:`What Pellridge costs, and what it says about itself`,
     setup:[`Pellridge's invoices for 2026 total $3,960,000. Meridian's total IT spending for 2026 was $11,000,000. The contract promises 99.95 percent availability for the settlement release service each month. Pellridge measures this itself and reports it monthly. Its February 2027 report, for a 28 day month, shows 9 minutes of downtime.`],
     fields:[{label:`Pellridge's share of Meridian's 2026 IT spending, in percent`,answer:36,tol:0},{label:`Downtime allowed by the SLA in a 30 day month, in minutes (one decimal)`,answer:21.6,tol:0.1},{label:`February 2027 availability, as Pellridge reports it, in percent (two decimals)`,answer:99.98,tol:0.01}],
     solution:[`Share = $3,960,000 ÷ $11,000,000 = 0.36, or 36 percent. More than a third of Meridian's IT spending went to one vendor that nobody at Meridian had ever reviewed.`,`A 30 day month has 30 × 24 × 60 = 43,200 minutes. At 99.95 percent the allowed downtime is 0.05 percent: 43,200 × 0.0005 = 21.6 minutes.`,`February 2027 has 28 × 24 × 60 = 40,320 minutes. Availability = (40,320 minus 9) ÷ 40,320 = 0.99978, or 99.98 percent. Comfortably inside the SLA, by Pellridge's own count. The arithmetic is right. Whether the 9 minutes are is a separate question, and the only way to answer it is with records Pellridge didn't write.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f4s6`, title:`Which label?`, intro:`Meridian's standard has four labels. Public: approved for release. Internal: for staff, low harm if it leaks. Confidential: sensitive business information for named groups. Restricted: misuse could move money or expose people's financial details. Dot would have made you label these before lunch.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Outgoing settlement files sent to the Federal Reserve.`, choices:[`Public`,`Internal`,`Confidential`,`Restricted`], answer:3, why:`Misuse could move money. Restricted, whatever the 2019 register said.`},
      {prompt:`Merchants' bank account and routing numbers.`, choices:[`Public`,`Internal`,`Confidential`,`Restricted`], answer:3, why:`Financial details that could be used to move money or defraud people. Restricted.`},
      {prompt:`Meridian's published fee schedule for merchants.`, choices:[`Public`,`Internal`,`Confidential`,`Restricted`], answer:0, why:`It's meant to be read by anyone considering Meridian. Public.`},
      {prompt:`The office cafeteria menu and the staff phone directory.`, choices:[`Public`,`Internal`,`Confidential`,`Restricted`], answer:1, why:`For staff, and little harm if it got out. Internal.`},
      {prompt:`Audit committee minutes discussing a pending acquisition.`, choices:[`Public`,`Internal`,`Confidential`,`Restricted`], answer:2, why:`Sensitive business information for a named group. Confidential. It can't move money, but it could move a share price.`},
      {prompt:`Meridian's draft fee proposals for next year's bank contracts.`, choices:[`Public`,`Internal`,`Confidential`,`Restricted`], answer:2, why:`Sensitive business information for the few people negotiating them. Confidential.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`A company will outsource part of its payment processing to a vendor with access to production systems. Which contract term is MOST important to the IS auditor?`, opts:[`A fixed fee for the whole contract term`,`A right to audit the vendor's controls`,`A named account manager at the vendor`,`Automatic renewal unless either side objects`], a:1, why:`A right to audit, or an agreed independent assurance report, is how the company keeps the ability to check controls it no longer runs itself. The other terms are commercial.`, task:`2B2`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`A company outsources the processing of its customers' data to a service provider. Who remains accountable for protecting that data?`, opts:[`The company that outsourced it`,`The service provider, under contract`,`The service provider's own auditors`,`The customers who supplied the data`], a:0, why:`Outsourcing moves the work, not the accountability. The company stays accountable, and manages that through due diligence, contract terms, and monitoring.`, task:`2B2`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`A vendor register marks a key IT provider "Related party: No", but evidence suggests a director may own it. What should the IS auditor do FIRST?`, opts:[`Report that the director owns the vendor`,`Ask the vendor who its owners are`,`Review the disclosures and approvals`,`Recommend ending the contract`], a:2, why:`A possible related party justifies testing, not a conclusion. Check the organization's related party disclosures, conflict of interest declarations, and contract approvals first.`, task:`2B2`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`What is the PRIMARY purpose of requiring staff in sensitive roles to take two consecutive weeks of leave?`, opts:[`To reduce the risk of staff burnout`,`To meet employment law requirements`,`To cut the cost of overtime payments`,`To let a substitute find irregularities`], a:3, why:`While someone else does the job, irregularities that need the regular person's daily presence tend to come to light. That's why banking regulators value the control.`, task:`2B1`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`Only one administrator knows how to run a critical nightly batch job. Which control BEST addresses this risk?`, opts:[`Cross training and written procedures`,`A higher salary for the administrator`,`More frequent reviews of the job logs`,`Moving the job to a newer server`], a:0, why:`Key person dependency is reduced by spreading the knowledge: cross training others and documenting the procedure. Pay, log reviews, and hardware don't remove the single point of failure.`, task:`2B1`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`Who should be responsible for classifying a set of business data?`, opts:[`The database administrator`,`The IS auditor on the engagement`,`The data owner in the business`,`The information security manager`], a:2, why:`The data owner, a business manager accountable for the data, decides its classification. IT and security act as custodians, and the auditor evaluates.`, task:`2A7`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`Which BEST describes the role of a data custodian?`, opts:[`Decides how sensitive the data is`,`Approves who may access the data`,`Uses the data to do their own job`,`Protects data as the owner directs`], a:3, why:`The custodian, usually IT, stores and protects data as the owner directs. Deciding sensitivity and approving access are the owner's job.`, task:`2A7`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`When should a privacy impact assessment be done for a new system that will process personal data?`, opts:[`After the first privacy complaint`,`During design, before it goes live`,`At the next scheduled annual audit`,`Once it has been running for a year`], a:1, why:`A privacy impact assessment belongs early, at design, so privacy risks are addressed before personal data is processed.`, task:`2A6`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`A company's privacy notice says it shares personal data only with providers bound by contract to protect it. One provider's contract has no such terms. What is the auditor's MAIN concern?`, opts:[`The notice is too long for most readers`,`The provider's fees were never benchmarked`,`The contract should have had board approval`,`The company isn't doing what it promised`], a:3, why:`A privacy notice is a commitment. A vendor contract without the promised terms means the company isn't meeting it, and the data at that vendor lacks the protections customers were told about.`, task:`2A6`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`An auditee offers an ISO 9001 certificate as evidence of quality over its payment system. What should the IS auditor check FIRST?`, opts:[`Whether its scope covers that system`,`The certification body's fees`,`How many staff hold ISO training`,`The date the company first applied`], a:0, why:`A certificate covers only its stated scope. If the payment system isn't within it, the certificate says nothing about that system.`, task:`2B4`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`A quality assurance team reports to the development manager whose releases it tests. What is the auditor's MAIN concern?`, opts:[`The team may be too small`,`Testing may delay releases`,`QA lacks independence`,`The manager needs QA training`], a:2, why:`QA should be independent of the work it reviews. Reporting to the manager whose output it tests compromises its objectivity.`, task:`2B4`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`In enterprise architecture, what does a gap analysis compare?`, opts:[`Budgeted and actual IT spending`,`Planned and actual project dates`,`Two vendors' proposals for a system`,`The current state and the target state`], a:3, why:`Gap analysis compares the architecture as it is with the architecture the organization has decided to reach. The gaps become the roadmap.`, task:`2A4`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`An IS auditor finds a vendor connection into production that doesn't appear in the documented architecture. What should the auditor do?`, opts:[`Add it to the diagram and move on`,`Report the gap and find who approved it`,`Ignore it, since diagrams are often old`,`Ask the vendor to disconnect it today`], a:1, why:`An undocumented connection into production is a finding. Report it and establish whether anyone approved it. Fixing the diagram or the connection is management's job.`, task:`2A4`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`A payments population includes a small group of high value items with very different risk. Which approach BEST gives that group enough coverage?`, opts:[`Stratified sampling`,`Stop or go sampling`,`Discovery sampling`,`Taking the first items`], a:0, why:`Stratification splits the population into groups that behave differently and samples each, so a small, important group isn't lost in a large one. Review of chapter 2.`, task:`1B2`, domain:`Auditing Process`},
    {q:`Which database feature makes sure a multi step update either completes in full or not at all?`, opts:[`Referential integrity constraints`,`Atomic transactions with rollback`,`A complete data dictionary`,`Read only views of tables`], a:1, why:`Transactions are atomic, all or nothing: if any step fails, the whole transaction rolls back. Referential integrity, the dictionary, and views do other jobs. Review of chapter 2.`, task:`4A11`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{const P=s.picks||{},told=!!(s.flags.toldkade||G.toldkade);
    return [
    ...(P[6]===3
      ?[`Giles had answered his email from the night before at 6:02 that morning. <b>Diary blocked, 29 March to 1 April, nothing before 9 or after 3 your time. Didn't know you had it in you. G.</b> Henry had forwarded it to Laura without a word, because it was the kind of thing she'd asked to be told.`]
      :[]),
    (told
      ?`At 1:35 counsel forwarded a letter from Meridian's general counsel. Meridian and Pellridge had, "in the ordinary course," signed an addendum to their 2019 agreement giving Meridian full audit rights. It was dated Monday, 4:15 PM, less than three hours after coffee. Anyone who read the contract from now on would find a right to audit in it. He made sure the copy counsel had sent him on Monday morning was in the file, with the time it arrived, and wrote underneath, in his own hand, what he'd said over coffee and to whom. Then he sent the same note to Giles himself, before anyone else could.`
      :`At 1:35 counsel rang to say Meridian had acknowledged the Pellridge requests and would respond "in due course." Nobody at Meridian had asked why he wanted them. As far as he could tell, nobody at Meridian knew.`),
    (s.score>=28
      ?`Every finding from the two days came back to the same door. A company nobody at Meridian had checked had held the keys to the release service since 2019, and in all that time the only person who'd looked up who owned it was him, this morning, for free.`
      :`Priyanka would read the two days by Thursday, and she'd send some of his calls back. He left them in the file as they were, which was more than anyone at Meridian had done for Pellridge.`),
    `At two his phone showed the Elmcroft number. It was eight in the evening in Muswell Hill, six hours away now instead of seven, and the call felt early, though it was exactly on time.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Precious">"He's asleep already, Henry. Long day. The hairdresser came, and he told her all about the Room."</span> A pause, and a page turning. <span class="said" data-who="Precious">"I went through the visitors' book, because of what he said last week. This year's, and last year's as well, to be sure. There's no Dorothy in either. No Kessler. Nobody from America at all."</span>`,
    `He wrote it down. Whatever his father knew about an American with a red folder, he hadn't learned it at Elmcroft.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Precious">"One other thing. It's not about the book."</span> She said it the way nurses say things they've already decided to say. <span class="said" data-who="Precious">"He asked for you at tea. I told him you were in America. He said, 'Still?'"</span>`,
    `Henry wrote that down too, out of habit, with the time beside it: 2:06 PM. Then he looked at it for a while, and drew a line through the time.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Precious">"Same time next week, then?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Same time,"</span> he said.`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch04.mp3`, len:`about 45 minutes`},
  next:`The Build`
});
